description: Android performance rules
alwaysApply: true
---

# Performance

Always generate efficient code.

## RecyclerView

Use

- `ListAdapter` + `DiffUtil.ItemCallback` (never `notifyDataSetChanged()`)
- Stable IDs when beneficial
- ViewBinding inside the ViewHolder, inflated once

Never

- Allocate objects, format dates, or read preferences inside `onBindViewHolder`
- Set click listeners that capture the position instead of `bindingAdapterPosition`

## Memory

- Avoid unnecessary object creation in loops and draw/bind paths.
- Clear ViewBinding in `onDestroyView` (BaseFragment already does this - do not keep your own copy of `binding`).
- Never hold a reference to an Activity, Fragment, View, or Context in a singleton, companion object, or static field.
- Cancel timers, handlers, listeners, and receivers in the matching lifecycle callback.

## UI

Never perform heavy work on the main thread.

Avoid expensive operations inside `onBindViewHolder`, `onDraw`, `onLayout`, and scroll listeners.

Flatten layouts instead of nesting; use `ConstraintLayout` chains and guidelines.

## Startup

Keep `MainApplication.onCreate` lightweight - theme and locale restore only.

Lazy initialize expensive components (`by lazy`, on first use).

Never do disk or network I/O on the main thread during startup.

## Images

Load through Glide (`helpers/ui/ImageHelper.kt`). Never decode bitmaps manually on the main thread.

## General

- Prefer efficient algorithms and collections.
- Avoid duplicate work and repeated resource lookups.
- Cache results when the input does not change.
- Keep rendering smooth (target 16ms frames).
